  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1206 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nMidday visible satellite imagery depicted a perfectly clear sky\nover SW KS. Perfect fall weather prevailed, with light and\nvariable winds less than 10 mph. Along with above normal\ntemperatures in the 60s, weather very atypical of November will\nbe enjoyed. \n\nA very weak dry cold front will provide a light northwest wind\nshift tonight with no impacts. Models project only minor cool\nadvection behind this cold front passage; as such, temperatures\nwill continue to warm easily through the 60s Wednesday\nafternoon. With NWly downslope masking whatever cool advection\nthere is, actually added 1-2 degrees to the NBM for max\ntemperatures Wednesday, more in line with 12z MAV guidance. \n\nA broad midlevel ridge axis, centered over the Great Basin\nWednesday, and along the spine of the Rockies Thursday, will\nspread onto the plains on Friday. Very few clouds are expected\nduring these three days, in a very dry subsident regime. \nEven NBM cranks out 0% sky grids Thursday. NBM and 12z MEX\nsuggest some cooling into the 50s Friday, but this appears\nartificial given the ridge axis overhead and associated\nthicknesses, along with elevated south winds and warm air\nadvection. A weak dry backdoor cold front on the 12z GFS \nwould explain Friday's cooler readings, but this front is absent\non other guidance. Regardless, quiet weather with no impacts\nwill continue through Friday. \n\nA longwave trough is expected to arrive over the weekend, but\nmodel guidance is still out of agreement regarding how strong\nthe trough will be when it arrives on the plains, the trough's\ntrack and orientation, and any resultant precipitation. NBM \npops in the chance/scattered category appear reasonable for now,\nand with no cold air available, all precipitation is expected \nto take the form of rain showers. 12z operational GFS depicts \nstrong dynamic lift and widespread rainfall Sunday, with QPF of \n0.25-0.75 inch. NBM probability of measurable QPF (> 0.01 inch) \nis currently near 50% across SW KS on Sunday. Even if wetter \nsolutions verify, no impacts from the rain are expected. \n\nFollowing this system, 12z MEX/GFS solutions seem too cold for\nMonday morning, suggesting 1030 mb cool surface ridging and\nsunrise temperatures in the 20s. With lingering cloud cover,\nwill carry 30s and 40s in the forecast, with still no killing \nfreezes in sight through mid November.",
